        "label": "Jean-Paul Sartre",
        "instagraph:type": "Philosopher",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8C8DC",
        "role": "contrasted with Camus; for Sartre absurdity precedes consciousness"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "kierkegaard",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "S\u00f8ren Kierkegaard",
        "instagraph:type": "Philosopher",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8C8DC",
        "role": "discovers and lives the absurd, but performs the leap to faith"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "nietzsche",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Friedrich Nietzsche",
        "instagraph:type": "Philosopher",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8C8DC",
        "role": "post-God absurdist forerunner; influences Camus's notion of the absurd"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "heidegger",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Martin Heidegger",
        "instagraph:type": "Philosopher",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8C8DC",
        "role": "centers existence on anguish; consciousness of death"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "jaspers",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Karl Jaspers",
        "instagraph:type": "Philosopher",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8C8DC",
        "role": "starts from failure of knowledge, leaps to transcendence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "shestov",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Lev Shestov",
        "instagraph:type": "Philosopher",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8C8DC",
        "role": "irrationalist; demonstrates rationalism stumbles on the irrational; leaps to God"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "husserl",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Edmund Husserl",
        "instagraph:type": "Philosopher",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8C8DC",
        "role": "founder of phenomenology; reinstates world's diversity, denies transcendent reason"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "aristotle",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Aristotle",
        "instagraph:type": "Philosopher",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8C8DC",
        "role": "cited for the contradiction at the root of self-reflective thought"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pascal",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Blaise Pascal",
        "instagraph:type": "Philosopher",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8C8DC",
        "role": "named diversion (eluding) as a structure of human evasion"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "schopenhauer",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Arthur Schopenhauer",
        "instagraph:type": "Philosopher",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8C8DC",
        "role": "praised suicide while seated at a well-set table \u2014 Camus's example of un-lived philosophy"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "dostoevsky",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Fyodor Dostoevsky",
        "instagraph:type": "Writer",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8D8C8",
        "role": "creator of Kirilov, who carries suicide-as-logical-conclusion"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "sophocles",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Sophocles",
        "instagraph:type": "Writer",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8D8C8",
        "role": "\u0152dipus is a model for the absurd victory through consciousness"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "homer",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Homer",
        "instagraph:type": "Writer",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8D8C8",
        "role": "preserves the Sisyphus myth; Sisyphus 'wisest of mortals'"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "myth_of_sisyphus",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "The Myth of Sisyphus",
        "instagraph:type": "Work",
        "instagraph:color": "#C8E0F8",
        "year": 1942,
        "role": "the essay; elucidates the absurd and refuses suicide"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "the_plague",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "The Plague",
        "instagraph:type": "Work",
        "instagraph:color": "#C8E0F8",
        "year": 1947,
        "role": "novel; the painful death of a child epitomizes the absurd"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "the_rebel",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "The Rebel",
        "instagraph:type": "Work",
        "instagraph:color": "#C8E0F8",
        "year": 1951,
        "role": "extends revolt into political ethic; opposes totalitarianism"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "absurd",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "the Absurd",
        "instagraph:type": "Concept",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8F0C8",
        "definition": "born of the confrontation between human longing for meaning and the silent, irrational world",
        "centrality": "the central object of the essay"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "suicide",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Suicide",
        "instagraph:type": "Concept",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8F0C8",
        "role": "Camus calls it 'the only truly serious philosophical problem'; ultimately rejected as a response to the absurd"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "lucidity",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Lucidity",
        "instagraph:type": "Concept",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8F0C8",
        "definition": "clarity of mind that refuses comforting illusions and self-deception; Camus's analogue to anguish"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "revolt",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Revolt",
        "instagraph:type": "Concept",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8F0C8",
        "role": "first consequence of the absurd: the conscious refusal to consent to it"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "absurd_freedom",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Absurd Freedom",
        "instagraph:type": "Concept",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8F0C8",
        "role": "second consequence: freedom from the illusion of metaphysical liberty; freedom of action without future"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "passion",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Passion",
        "instagraph:type": "Concept",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8F0C8",
        "role": "third consequence: maximizing quantity and intensity of conscious experience"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "hope",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Hope",
        "instagraph:type": "Concept",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8F0C8",
        "role": "the chief form of eluding; named as the third theme of the essay; rejected by absurd reasoning"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "death",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Death",
        "instagraph:type": "Concept",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8F0C8",
        "role": "the irreparable; the cruel mathematics that crowns the absurd"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "consciousness",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Consciousness",
        "instagraph:type": "Concept",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8F0C8",
        "role": "everything begins with consciousness; awakening from mechanical life inaugurates the absurd"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "reason",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Reason",
        "instagraph:type": "Concept",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8F0C8",
        "role": "the rationalist project; not negated absolutely, but shown to founder on the irrational"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "irrational",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "the Irrational",
        "instagraph:type": "Concept",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8F0C8",
        "role": "the unreasonable silence of the world; one of the two terms of the absurd"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "nostalgia_for_unity",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Nostalgia for Unity",
        "instagraph:type": "Concept",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8F0C8",
        "role": "the human appetite for the absolute and for clarity; the other term of the absurd"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "anguish",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Anguish (Anxiety)",
        "instagraph:type": "Concept",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8F0C8",
        "role": "Heidegger's perpetual climate of the lucid mind; sibling concept to Camus's lucidity"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "leap",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "the Leap",
        "instagraph:type": "Concept",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8F0C8",
        "role": "the existential move from absurd to faith / God / transcendence; Camus's name for the betrayal of the absurd"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "philosophical_suicide",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Philosophical Suicide",
        "instagraph:type": "Concept",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8F0C8",
        "role": "Camus's term for the leap: existential thought presupposes the absurd only to dispel it"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "eluding",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Eluding (Diversion)",
        "instagraph:type": "Concept",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8F0C8",
        "role": "Pascalian; the pretense of a future, a meaning, or an other-life that masks the present condition"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "quantity_over_quality",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Quantity over Quality",
        "instagraph:type": "Concept",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8F0C8",
        "role": "Don Juan's ethic; substitute the most living for the best living"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "transcendence",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Transcendence",
        "instagraph:type": "Concept",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8F0C8",
        "role": "Jaspers's leap-target; what failure paradoxically reveals"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "god",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "God (the Absurd's terminus)",
        "instagraph:type": "Concept",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8F0C8",
        "role": "Shestov's leap-target; identified with the absurd itself in mystical readings; Camus's contested object"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "the_confrontation",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "the Confrontation",
        "instagraph:type": "Concept",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8F0C8",
        "role": "the unceasing struggle between human nostalgia and the silent world; the absurd is born of it, not in either term"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "absurd_man",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "the Absurd Man",
        "instagraph:type": "Archetype",
        "instagraph:color": "#DCC8F8",
        "role": "the figure who lives the absurd without the leap: lucid, revolting, free, passionate"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "absurd_hero",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "the Absurd Hero",
        "instagraph:type": "Archetype",
        "instagraph:color": "#DCC8F8",
        "role": "the form taken by Sisyphus, \u0152dipus, Don Juan: superior to fate through consciousness"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "don_juan",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Don Juan",
        "instagraph:type": "Archetype",
        "instagraph:color": "#DCC8F8",
        "role": "Kierkegaard's 'Don Juan of the understanding' figure; archetype of quantity-over-quality"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "oedipus",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "\u0152dipus",
        "instagraph:type": "LiteraryFigure",
        "instagraph:color": "#C8F8DC",
        "source": "Sophocles",
        "role": "concludes 'all is well'; ancient counterpart of the absurd victory"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "hamlet",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Hamlet",
        "instagraph:type": "LiteraryFigure",
        "instagraph:color": "#C8F8DC",
        "source": "Shakespeare",
        "role": "'the time is out of joint' \u2014 quoted by Shestov as scaffold for hope, by Camus as evidence of the absurd"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "kirilov",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Kirilov",
        "instagraph:type": "LiteraryFigure",
        "instagraph:color": "#C8F8DC",
        "source": "Dostoevsky, Demons",
        "role": "carried suicide as logical conclusion of refusing meaning to life"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "sisyphus",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Sisyphus",
        "instagraph:type": "MythologicalFigure",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8C8B8",
        "source": "Greek myth; Homer",
        "role": "wisest of mortals; chained Death; defied the gods; condemned to roll the rock; the absurd hero"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pluto",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Pluto",
        "instagraph:type": "MythologicalFigure",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8C8B8",
        "role": "ruler of the underworld; condemned Sisyphus"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mercury",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Mercury",
        "instagraph:type": "MythologicalFigure",
        "instagraph:color": "#F8C8B8",
        "role": "seized Sisyphus and returned him to the underworld"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "the_rock",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "the Rock",
        "instagraph:type": "Object",
        "instagraph:color": "#E8DCC0",
        "role": "Sisyphus's burden; symbol of futile and hopeless labor; what the conscious hero is stronger than"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "existentialism",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Existentialism",
        "instagraph:type": "Movement",
        "instagraph:color": "#E0E0E0",
        "role": "the philosophical movement Camus is in dialogue with \u2014 and accuses of philosophical suicide"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "phenomenology",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Phenomenology",
        "instagraph:type": "Movement",
        "instagraph:color": "#E0E0E0",
        "role": "Husserl's method; reinstates world's diversity; denies transcendent reason"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "christianity",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Christianity",
        "instagraph:type": "Movement",
        "instagraph:color": "#E0E0E0",
        "role": "the religious tradition Kierkegaard leaps into; one of the leap's named destinations"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "totalitarianism",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Totalitarianism",
        "instagraph:type": "Movement",
        "instagraph:color": "#E0E0E0",
        "role": "fascist or communist; named in The Rebel as the most horrifying enemy of revolt"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "bourgeois_morality",
      "attrs": {
        "label": "Bourgeois Morality",
        "instagraph:type": "Movement",
        "instagraph:color": "#E0E0E0",
        "role": "named in The Rebel as the more obvious enemy of lucid revolt"
      }
    }
